package watoots:preview@0.1.0;

/// A file-format decoder as a plugin: untrusted bytes in, pixels out.
///
/// This is the trade the asset world deliberately refuses -- there, the host
/// decodes at the edge and hands plugins pixels that are already safe. Here the
/// codec *is* the plugin, parsing input the user got from the internet, which
/// is the most exploited surface in native software and the one every desktop
/// application eventually opens to third parties. That is exactly why it
/// belongs in a sandbox, and why a decoder is the example that shows what the
/// sandbox is for.
interface types {
  /// Decoded pixels, RGBA8, row-major, `width * height * 4` bytes.
  ///
  /// A host must check that length before trusting it. A decoder that returns
  /// fewer bytes than its dimensions claim is the kind of thing an untrusted
  /// plugin must not be able to make a host act on.
  record image {
    width: u32,
    height: u32,
    pixels: list<u8>,
  }

  /// Why a decode did not produce an image.
  ///
  /// `not-this-format` is the host's dispatch signal -- try the next decoder --
  /// and must be cheap to reach: a decoder answers it from the magic bytes,
  /// before allocating anything. The other two are about *this* file.
  variant failure {
    not-this-format,
    /// The stream ended early; the payload is how many more bytes were needed.
    truncated(u64),
    /// The stream is malformed in a way the decoder can name.
    corrupt(string),
  }
}

/// One decoder. A host registers several and dispatches on `sniff`.
///
/// Nothing is imported: a decoder needs no filesystem, no network, no clock.
/// Under a manifest granting nothing at all it still works, and a host that
/// reads `[permissions]` and finds it empty has read the whole security
/// argument for keeping codecs out of the process.
///
/// Deliberately *not* asked to police its own resource use. A decoder cannot
/// know the host's memory budget, so a header claiming an image too large to
/// be real is the *sandbox's* to refuse, via `limits.memory`, not the plugin's.
/// A plugin that is careful is nice; a sandbox that does not need it to be is
/// the point.
world decoder {
  use types.{image, failure};

  /// The format this decodes, e.g. `"qoi"`. For the host's plugin list.
  export format: func() -> string;

  /// Whether `prefix` -- the first few bytes of a file -- looks like this
  /// format. Answered from magic bytes alone; must not allocate.
  export sniff: func(prefix: list<u8>) -> bool;

  /// Decode a whole file.
  export decode: func(data: list<u8>) -> result<image, failure>;
}
